# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres connection
#
# Any Postgres works. Neon's free tier is what this was built against:
#   https://console.neon.tech  ->  your project  ->  Connection string
#
# Copy this file to .env and paste your connection string below. When deployed,
# the app reads the same variable from Streamlit Cloud secrets instead, so this
# file is local development only. It is gitignored.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/talent_market?sslmode=require


# ── Use the DIRECT connection string, not the pooled one ─────────────────────
#
# Neon offers both. The pooled endpoint has "-pooler" in the hostname and
# routes through PgBouncer in transaction mode. Avoid it here, for two reasons:
#
#   1. psycopg3 automatically starts preparing a statement once it has been
#      executed a handful of times, and prepared statements do not survive
#      PgBouncer's transaction pooling. Every query in this app repeats.
#
#   2. This app already pools connections itself (see tms/db.py). Stacking a
#      second pooler underneath adds a failure mode and buys nothing.
#
# If your hostname contains "-pooler", you copied the wrong one.
#
# Should you ever need the pooled endpoint anyway, disable prepared statements:
#   DATABASE_URL=...?sslmode=require&prepare_threshold=0


# ── Notes on the free tier ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Neon suspends compute after a few minutes idle, so the first query of a
# session takes a couple of seconds to wake it. That is expected, not an error
# — tms/db.py allows 15 seconds to connect and validates pooled connections
# before use, so a suspended database reconnects rather than failing.


# ── Local Postgres instead? ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Point at it directly; no other config changes:
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/talent_market
